{"title":"2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">Problem 3</a></span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Galois_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For every positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, the units digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/4/e941fa1f65771279f81442b4db498594d4cd778a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{1}, a^{2}, a^{3}, a^{4}, a^{5}, \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"152\" height=\"20\" /> will form a repeating sequence. In each such sequence, the smallest number of consecutive units digits that repeat consecutively and indefinitely is at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This number is called the <i>cycle length</i>. For example, when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/b/33b3c27171aba2140505212ce5b912115d1cdd69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />,\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e78a015754b330e041e88008840d8c7e8d6e23b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^{1} = 3, 3^{2} = 9, 3^{3} = 27, 3^{4} = 81, 3^{5} = 243, 3^{6} = 729, \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"428\" height=\"20\" /></center>\n<p>and the sequence of units digits is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/d/1ddd47c2bdc55b725b35c93fb1a9a144a2f4db42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 9, 7, 1, 3, 9, \\cdots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"17\" />. In this example, the consecutive units digits that repeat are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/5/5355694be699c9a6dd49a99e0a8fee0ce4e963f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 9, 7, 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" />, and so the cycle length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Parts\">Parts</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Galois_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Parts\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Galois_Problems/Problem_3a\" title=\"2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3a\">2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3a</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Galois_Problems/Problem_3b&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3b (page does not exist)\">2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3b</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_CEMC_Galois_Problems/Problem_3c&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3c (page does not exist)\">2026 CEMC Galois Problems/Problem 3c</a></li></ul>\n\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018034715\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.009 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.013 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 51/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nU